  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "The study conducted large-scale empirical experiments across approximately 100 highly congested road segments in 10 major US cities, rerouting a small share of Google Maps trips to alternative routes of equivalent road classes. This marginal routing intervention achieved a city-average increase in vehicle speeds of 2% on the targeted segments, improved travel times by 0.7%, and showed potential to reduce annual CO2-equivalent emissions by over 1,000 tons per city in most locations.",
      "why": "It provides real-world empirical evidence that minor, app-based routing adjustments affecting only a small fraction of drivers can measurably improve overall urban road network efficiency without requiring infrastructure changes."
